05 — Pi-hole Setup

What is this for?
Pi-hole is a network-wide ad blocker that acts as a DNS server for your home network. Instead of blocking ads on a per-device basis, all DNS queries on your network go through Pi-hole first. It blocks known ad and tracking domains before they ever reach your devices.

Prerequisites
Portainer is running on the Ubuntu Server VM
The Ubuntu Server VM has a static IP or a DHCP reservation set on your router

Setting a Static IP (Recommended)
Pi-hole works best when the host machine always has the same IP address.
The easiest way is to set a DHCP reservation on your router — assign the Ubuntu Server VM's MAC address a fixed IP (e.g. 10.0.0.x).

Deploying Pi-hole in Portainer
Open Portainer at https://10.0.0.x:9443
Go to Stacks → Add Stack
Name it pihole
Paste the following into the Web editor:

   version: "3"
   services:
     pihole:
       image: pihole/pihole:latest
       container_name: pihole
       restart: unless-stopped
       ports:
         - "53:53/tcp"
         - "53:53/udp"
         - "80:80/tcp"
       environment:
         TZ: America/New_York
         WEBPASSWORD: yourpasswordhere
       volumes:
         - "/home/yourusername/pihole/etc-pihole:/etc/pihole"
         - "/home/yourusername/pihole/etc-dnsmasq.d:/etc/dnsmasq.d"
       dns:
         - 127.0.0.1
         - 1.1.1.1

Replace yourpasswordhere with a strong password for the Pi-hole web UI.
Replace yourusername with your actual Ubuntu username.
Replace America/New_York with your timezone if different.

Click Deploy the stack

Accessing Pi-hole
Open a browser and navigate to:

   http://10.0.0.x/admin

Log in with the password you set in WEBPASSWORD.

Pointing Your Network to Pi-hole
For Pi-hole to block ads network-wide, your devices need to use it as their DNS server.

Option 1 — Router DNS (Recommended, covers all devices)
Log in to your router admin page
Find the DNS settings (usually under LAN or DHCP settings)
Set the Primary DNS to your Ubuntu Server VM's IP (e.g. 10.0.0.x)
Save and reboot the router

Option 2 — Per device
Manually set DNS to 10.0.0.x on each device you want covered

Verify It is Working
Open the Pi-hole dashboard at http://10.0.0.x/admin
You should start seeing DNS queries appear on the dashboard within a few minutes
The Total Queries and Queries Blocked counters should increase as devices on your network browse the web

